<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Dumnonii</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 55 CE (approx.) · capital Isca Dumnoniorum (Exeter)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iron Age people of Devon and Cornwall, whose tin and remote farming settlements kept them apart from the coin-using kingdoms of the south-east.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dumnonii emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> communities of the south-western peninsula, a land of hillforts, defended farmsteads known as rounds, and coastal promontory forts. Greek writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pytheas">Pytheas</a> onwards describe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> trade of the far south-west, and harbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-batten">Mount Batten</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a> linked the region with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Brittany</a> and the wider Atlantic world.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>No kings or coins of the Dumnonii are known, which suggests a looser society of local lineages rather than a single dynasty. Most people lived in small enclosed farms raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and grain, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> streaming in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dartmoor">Dartmoor</a> gave the region its main export. Courtyard houses and underground passages called fogous are distinctive of the far west.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Roman forces reached the south-west after 43, and around 55 the legio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">II Augusta</a> built a fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>. When the army moved on, the town became Isca Dumnoniorum, capital of a self-governing civitas, though the countryside stayed little Romanised. The name survived in the post-<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dumnonia">Dumnonia</a> and in the county name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dumnonii.jpg" alt="Dumnonii" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.south.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 55 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Isca Dumnoniorum (Exeter)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dumnonians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-22&amp;focus=dumnonii" aria-label="Dumnonii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4600 417 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dumnonii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4920 673L5304 942M4920 673L5020 640M4920 673L4920 673"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0M4920 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4920 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4920 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Isca Dumnoniorum. <a href="/atlas?year=-22&amp;focus=dumnonii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
